            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "mismicma"
            },
            "reviewBody": "For anyone who has not made a roux before, heat the oil and flour over LOW heat--the recipe does not specify---but you need to be careful with the hot oil and adding water. Mine was a little too hot and almost bubbled over. \n\nAll warnings aside, this was an excellent meal. The eggs were delicious and used in a way that was new to me.\n\nI did substitute broth for the water."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Cliff"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I'm not sure I got the roux right but the final product was delicious so it couldn't have been too far off. I used chicken stock instead of water and also used a pound of andouille in place of one of the pounds of shrimp. I'd suggest browning the sausage first before adding to the gumbo. Would definitely make this again.",
            "datePublished": "2013-12-16T09:48:59.250Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jason Cross"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I didn't expect to cook the best gumbo I had ever had myself and I didn't expect eggs to feature in it either. I used large shrimp with tails and, while I little more of a pain to eat, it was spectacular. I didn't have the bell peppers or okra on hand, however the flavor was still spectacular. Make sure to use freshly cracked black pepper.",
            "datePublished": "2012-04-08T09:57:51.800Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Pete"
            },
            "reviewBody": "The recipe was great and easy to make, but it was lacking a certain taste. Using broth instead of water would certainly improve this recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2011-11-28T16:01:45.273Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "tonya"
            },
            "reviewBody": "VERY good! I am very picky about my gumbo. Some people make 'gumbo' and us Louisianians would call it 'soup'. I use boiled eggs in mine, especially in the winter time. They make it heartier and very filling.",
            "datePublished": "2009-11-18T06:58:12.303Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ashleyf"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was a pretty easy, basic gumbo recipe and was delicious. The addition of eggs is something new (and very french) and gave a new take on the traditional gumbo. My only advice is to make sure your cook the roux perfectly...it can ruin your recipe if not!",
            "datePublished": "2009-03-16T08:12:02.777Z"
          }
